WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.509
Film Courage: Can you give me an example of
just showing, whether it’s someone waking

00:00:02.509 --> 00:00:04.330
up in the morning and they have back pain…

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:12.450
Peter Russell: Oh yeah…BREAKING BAD, here
is this horrible guy, he got rid of his brother-in-law

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:16.570
(we didn’t mind seeing him die), but he
ends up ruining his family entirely, Jesse,

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:21.930
etc. When we first see Walter White, actually
we first see him in his underwear in the desert,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:27.810
when we first see him at home waking up, he’s
waking up in this awful little bedroom with

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:35.820
a terrible nightstand, he’s got this crappy
little exercise machine which he gets up on

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.670
which he’s broken after 10 seconds (one
of those step things) and then the camera

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:50.150
pans to the wall and we see on the wall that
in 1983 Walter White was in line for a Nobel

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:58.800
Prize in Physics (Chemistry). Then we go back
to him and see he’s dead, something has

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:06.450
hurt him. What is it? Well we find out much
later what it is, that he was betrayed by

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.249
his partner, by his love. And then he goes
and he slumps down and he is sitting at his

00:01:11.249 --> 00:01:16.499
breakfast table and it’s his 50th birthday
and he gets soy bacon for his birthday. And

00:01:16.499 --> 00:01:22.490
his wife goes “Eat it! You’ll like it!”
And then his son comes out and says “Well,

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:26.810
the water heater is not working.” “Well,
you’ve got to get up early and be the first

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.939
in the shower.” “Why can’t we buy a
new water heater?” Right? Well, because

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:33.759
Walter is a loser, right?

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:41.130
Everything about Walter shows us that he’s
been horribly wounded by something. What we’re

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:47.439
rooting for is for Walter to get better and
so when Walter becomes a badass, I don’t

00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:52.159
know about you but I’m on his side. I know
he’s hurting innocent people, I don’t

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:55.520
care…”Walter, get ‘em, get ‘em! Get
that guy!”

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:02.429
So that is a terrible part of the human soul
baby. But it’s the device by which we make

00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:07.209
a character sympathetic is to show their wounds
because as human beings we’re not going

00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:11.629
to be interested in good-looking, perfect
people who are making a lot of money and they

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:17.000
are great in everything they do. Who gives
a crap? We want to see people that we can

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.909
identify with because that’s not us. We’ve
got problems, right? I’ve got problems,

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:29.370
right? I want to see my problems and somebody
else with problems, dealing with problems,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.370
okay?

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:39.319
And you can say Okay a show like RIVERDALE
doesn’t do that, but they do. And there’s

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:45.150
fantasy shows where that’s not the case.
But most of the time you do want to see a

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:50.219
wound. That’s what likability really means.
“Oh, they’re like me? They’re screwed

00:02:50.219 --> 00:02:55.829
up. They don’t have it all together. Wish
I did. Maybe they’ll get it all together,

00:02:55.829 --> 00:02:56.829
right?

00:02:56.829 --> 00:02:58.420
Film Courage: And then that means “I’ll
get it together.”

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:03.340
Peter: Uh-huh. How did they get it together?
But mostly it’s just like “Yeah, they’re

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.719
like me. They’re not perfect, they are like
me.”

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:10.230
Film Courage: Well, you’ve been using an
acronym for a little bit. And what’s funny

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.662
is driving over I was looking through the
notes and I think that David [Branin] had

00:03:13.662 --> 00:03:18.870
said off camera that BMOC stands for Big Man
On Campus in the basketball world which I

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:24.630
was not privy to, I did not know that. For
writing what is your take on this acronym?

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:28.610
Peter: Beginning, middle, obstacle and climax.
That’s what that stands for. I found this

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:34.790
out years ago analyzing all movies that there
was an E = mc2 moment for me. There were four

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:39.829
crescendos in a movie where the hero is asked
to change and asked to learn the theme of

00:03:39.829 --> 00:03:45.180
the movie and asked to learn how to heal.
All the things I talk about, the big things

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.981
I talk about, healing, learning the theme,
stopping bleeding, all that. There’s four

00:03:50.981 --> 00:03:58.010
times in a movie inevitably that that happens
at a crescendo. It’s 30 pages in, 60 pages

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:03.810
in, 90 pages in and about a 108 pages in.
Those I call the beginning, middle and climax.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:09.659
The BMOC, right? Now that’s a structure
that is in every great movie practically that

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:13.329
you’ve ever seen. It’s not in a [Jean-Luc]
Godard movie, okay. If you’re writing a

00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:16.729
French Wave movie, I’m sorry I won’t be
able to help you. That’s just a French guy

00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:21.709
peeing in an alley for two hours and that’s
great! I love those movies.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:29.849
But in a Hollywood film that structure is
invariably in the story and if it’s not

00:04:29.849 --> 00:04:34.120
there’s usually something missing. It will
be superseded some day but that’s what’s

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.250
operating now.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:46.210
That BMOC operates in everything, every movie
DUNKIRK, in DEADPOOL, everything. But now

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:52.660
in DEADPOOL (let’s just take an example)
which is a great movie again I’m big on

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.819
wounds, right? What’s our wound in DEADPOOL?
The guy…well he’s wounded because he’s

00:04:56.819 --> 00:05:03.030
ugly. He becomes extremely disfigured by a
chemical bath right. And because of his wound

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:10.600
I’m ugly and he can’t face his girl. He
doesn’t feel lovable. So the big crescendos

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:17.340
(the BMOC) points in that movie are is he
going to have the balls or the courage to

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:23.099
believe that his girl will love him even though
he’s ugly.

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:27.190
So what he’s got to learn in there to heal
his wound is I’m lovable. His wound is “Im

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:32.379
not lovable.” Right? And that’s a wound
we can all identify with. A lot of us think

00:05:32.379 --> 00:05:38.180
we’re not lovable. So his journey in DEADPOOL,
the crescendos in the movie are you going

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:44.890
to realize you’re lovable. The answer for
him is “Yes, no, no, no, yes. Yes, no, no,

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:51.900
no, yes.” Because after he gets chemically
doused he’s too ugly and it takes him most

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:57.270
of the movie to where he can take his mask
off, that’s the climax, right? That’s

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.630
the part where he takes the mask off. I use
DEADPOOL, I could use a more high-flouted

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:05.540
movie but I want people to understand this
pattern of BMOC works in every movie. And

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:09.350
you can say “Wow, that’s great Peter,
that’s a tool, so what?” The tool will

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:12.889
help you write your movie, you’ve got to
figure out what your theme is, you’ve got

00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:15.000
to figure out what your character’s wound
is and you’ve got to figure out how they

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.710
are going to be healing it. That’s it, those
are the three things. So the BMOC does that.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:25.879
Now the interesting thing is a few years ago
I freaked out and nearly lost my stuff because

00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:26.879
I thought “Gosh, movies are going to be
over. Television is everything, oh my Gosh.

00:06:26.879 --> 00:06:36.600
I’m a movie guy, I don’t know what to
do?”

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:43.710
So I slowly started tentatively “Does this
work? Does this work? Okay, that works? Does

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:49.879
the BMOC works?” Well the BMOC turns out
to work in television as well. So this tool

00:06:49.879 --> 00:06:59.340
works in drama and comedy and TV as well (because
why?) Because what we want to see in a story

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:05.581
is the hero learning to heal and even if he
doesn’t heal completely because he doesn’t

00:07:05.581 --> 00:07:10.949
in TV, he’s still going to go on a journey
to healing a little bit. The critical difference

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:15.590
between film and television is that in TV
the hero doesn’t heal but he heals a little

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:20.460
bit and at the end he gets ripped open again.
So every week he’s going to heal a little

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.220
bit and get ripped open again, he’s going
to heal a little bit and get ripped open again,

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:29.430
every week (152 times). But the BMOC works
at the exact same place as in a television

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:38.300
hour as it does in a movie, there’s just
an extra M because there’s an extra act.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:46.800
So let’s take a 4-act television structure,
it’s B M M O C. The O C (the obstacle point)

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.250
is the point where it looks like the hero
is going to lose. And in a movie this is right

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:56.090
at the 90-page point, let’s take a movie
everybody knows (STAR WARS). Luke gets to

00:07:56.090 --> 00:08:01.130
the planet (the last rebel planet), Darth
has followed him there with a homing beacon,

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:04.520
he’s got a death star, he’s going to blow
up the whole damn thing. There is nothing

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.639
Luke can do about it, there’s just a little
star fighters and he’s got a death star.

00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:10.909
That’s it, the movie is over.

00:08:10.909 --> 00:08:16.310
And of course what happens is R2D2 says “Hey,
I stole the plans of the Death Star, there’s

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:20.520
a little hole you can fill a missile in if
you get close. And Luke you can be the only

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:24.840
one to get that missile in that hole if you
use The Force, not your computer. And so the

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:32.080
obstacle point is all is lost and the climax
is Luke learns the theme of the story, Luke

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:37.480
will you use the force to rescue the princess
and save The Republic. He puts his computer

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:42.180
away, all the other star righters used it.
They couldn’t get the missile in, he’s

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:47.970
learned, he hears Obi-Wan say “Luke, use
The Force, not the computer!” And he puts

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:50.810
the missile in the hole, climax.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:56.330
So the obstacle point is “All is lost”
and the climax is “Luke you did it.”

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:02.280
In BREAKING BAD, the obstacle point is he’s
making bad things in the dessert, Jesse comes

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:07.710
back out with the Crazy Eight and the other
criminal, they’re going to hurt them both.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:13.640
Walt is in his underwear, he has no defense.
He’s going to get harmed. But he says “I’ll

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:20.520
teach you, I’ll teach you how to cook my
recipe.” And the guy goes “Really?”

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:21.710
“Yes.”

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:26.930
And the go in the RV and Walter throws poison
into the thing “Whoosh!” And the climax

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:35.220
is he kills them both and becomes the mass
for the next 150 episodes. So that’s the

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:39.980
climax. So the O and the C point (I’m not
explaining this well but it’s in my lecture

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:46.600
online), it’s a complex tool but it works
great, it’s like a little engine that works

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:51.850
any television structure you want, just like
it did in the film. So what did I learn when

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:57.790
I started learning about television, everything
I learned in movies works.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.600
Every element that I learned in movies works
in television, the structure is just a little

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:03.830
more complicated, that’s all.

